# EXPECT: rebinds after this definition
#
# A nested `define` capturing a name the enclosing function later REBINDS.
#
# THE POINT OF THIS FIXTURE is that the lift pass's own design comment claimed
# this case throws — "Numeric captures would SNAPSHOT (env copy of a double),
# so they throw" — and no such throw was ever written. The capture is bound at
# the definition site, so a later rebind is invisible:
#
#   local x is 100 / define inner(d) as: print of x / x is 7 / inner of 1
#   VM   7        AOT  100        both build, both rc 0, no diagnostic
#
# The comment was also wrong about the SCOPE of the hazard. It said numeric
# captures; a string rebind snapshots identically (VM "b", AOT "a"). What it
# gets right is the other half — mutation THROUGH a capture stays live, because
# the Value is aliased — which is why `box[0] is 9` is not refused and is
# covered by its sibling parity fixture.
#
# THE SUITE HAD ZERO NESTED-DEFINE FIXTURES when this was found, which is how
# 125/125 stayed green over it. A `for` binder counts as a rebind too: HEAD's
# own genp binder writes the C variable, which a snapshot capture never sees.

define f(y) as:
    local x is 100
    define inner(d) as:
        print of x
        return 0
    x is 7
    print of (inner of 1)
    return 0

print of (f of 0)
